{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>וְלָקַח הַכֹּהֵן מַיִם קְדֹשִׁים בִּכְלִי חָרֶשׂ — And the priest shall take holy water in an earthen vessel.</b> According to the straightforward reading, the matter of this test seems to me as follows: in every place where you find a boundary of forbidden sexual relations, there you find holiness. Now these waters, which were sanctified in the kiyyor, and into which the Name that was erased was placed — they surely possess extraordinary holiness. And every liquid issues by way of the womb through the urine. Therefore, if she became defiled and there was a boundary of forbidden relations, it is not fitting that these holy waters should touch the place of forbidden relations that became defiled. Therefore her belly swells and her thigh falls: for such is nature in every illness of the urinary tract, that the liquid held back inside a person with no way to exit acts in one of two ways, or both together — namely, his belly swells, for the fluids remaining in the belly with no way out cause distension of the belly; or the liquid makes itself another path, which is that it is absorbed into the flesh of the thigh. And since that is not its proper place, it must of necessity cause the flesh of the thigh to drop, and together with the pieces of flesh that fall, the liquid also drains through there. But if she is pure and that place was not defiled, then the organs of intercourse are like the rest of a person's organs and there is no aspect of impurity in them — for behold, before the first man sinned it is written (Genesis 3:25): ״וַיִּהְיוּ הָאָדָם וְאִשְׁתּוֹ עֲרוּמִּים וְלֹא יִתְבֹּשָׁשׁוּ — and they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed,״ for as long as they had no inclination toward sin, the organs of intercourse were to them like other organs, in which there is no shame when they are exposed. So too, when there is a boundary of forbidden relations, the liquid issues normally and is of no concern. And because she sinned through the liquid of wine, therefore she is tested through the liquid of water." "And that they take earth from the floor of the Mishkan and put it into it — this is explained by way of what I wrote above in parashat Aḥarei Mot (Leviticus 18:25) on the verse ״וַתִּטְמָא הָאָרֶץ וָאֶפְקֹד עֲוֹנָהּ עָלֶיהָ — and the land became defiled, and I will visit its iniquity upon it,״ that in every place where there is the sin of forbidden relations, the first sin committed with the tree that makes fruit rather than a fruit-tree is recalled upon the earth — for by it, coarse and thick matter was introduced into all created beings, and along with this it also introduced into man coarse and turbid matter that causes him to have a particularly strong inclination toward sexual licentiousness, as the Ramban explained in parashat Bereshit (Genesis 2:9), that eating of the tree caused him to desire intercourse. And it is known that the first man was created from the place of his atonement — from the place of which it is said ״מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַ֢שֶׂה לִּי — an altar of earth you shall make for Me״ (Exodus 20:21), and that is from the earth of the Mishkan. And this matter is present in all his descendants, whose source is from that first matter. If so, the earth that is in the floor of the Mishkan is the artisan who made that vessel — that is, the human being. Woe to the dough that the baker testifies against it! For that earth can testify concerning the vessel, whether He placed in it coarse and thick matter that causes sexual licentiousness or not, and the artisan may come and testify concerning the vessel He made — what its nature is." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory disagreed in this matter, whether there was any bitter substance in these waters. For according to our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sotah 20a), a bitter substance was placed in them; but the view of the Ramban is that since it is not written ״וּבָאוּ בָהּ הַמַּיִם הַמָּרִים לַמְאָרְרִים — and the bitter waters that cause bitterness shall come into her,״ but is written in reversed order, ״וּבָאוּ הַמַּיִם הַמְאָרְרִים לְמָרִים — and the waters that cause bitterness shall come to be bitter,״ hear from this that there was no bitter substance in them, but only that in her mouth they became bitter. And so we find with the waters of Marah, of which it is written (Exodus 15:23): ״וְלֹא יָכְלוּ לִשְׁתּוֹת מַיִם מִמָּרָה כִּי מָרִים הֵם — and they could not drink water from Marah, for they were bitter״ — and it is not written ״for they were bitter״ (using הָיוּ), but rather that they were not bitter, only that in their mouths they turned bitter; therefore it says ״כִּי מָרִים הֵם״ — that is, Israel were bitter. And because the place is called ״Marah,״ which implies that bitter waters had always been there, therefore it says ״עַל כֵּן קָרָא שְׁמָהּ מָרָה — therefore one called its name Marah,״ on account of the event that occurred there, that these waters turned bitter in their mouths by way of punishment." "And what seems close to me to say regarding this: the waters of Marah and the waters of the sotah and the matter of the calf are all one thing, since our Rabbis of blessed memory already said (Mekhilta Yitro, chapter 8:13) that the Decalogue were five opposite five, and the utterance ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ — you shall not have״ corresponds to ״לֹא תִנְאָף — you shall not commit adultery,״ for one who serves idolatry is as if he commits adultery against the Place, as it is written (Ezekiel 16:32): ״הָאִשָּׁה הַמְּנָאָפֶת — the adulterous woman,״ etc. And at Marah they were steeped in idolatry, as is found in the Mekhilta (Beshalaḥ [VaYissa] 1:22), and the Yalkut brings it in parashat Beshalaḥ (remez 254) on the verse ״וַיַּסַּע מֹשֶׁה אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל — and Moses caused Israel to journey,״ where they said there: Let us make for ourselves an idolatry and it will go at our head. One might think they said it but did not do it — Scripture therefore says: ״וַיְמָאֲנוּ לִשְׁמֹעַ״ etc. (Nehemiah 9:17) — see there. And according to this midrash, they were there like an adulterous woman: just as sweet waters turn bitter in her mouth, so the waters turned bitter in their mouths. ״וַיּוֹרֵהוּ ה׳ עֵץ — and YHWH showed him a tree״: He taught him the way of Torah, which is a tree of life that distances idolatry, and through this ״וַיִּמְתְּקוּ הַמָּיִם — and the waters became sweet.״ And this is the matter of the calf, of which it is written (Exodus 32:20) ״וַיִּטְחַן עַד אֲשֶׁר דָּק — and he ground it until it was fine,״ etc., ״וַיַּשְׁקְ אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל — and he made the children of Israel drink,״ and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Avodah Zarah 44a) that he intended to test them like the waters of the sotah, etc. — both because idolaters are judged like a sotah, and because they transgressed at that time also the prohibition of forbidden sexual relations, as they derived (Tanhuma Ki Tisa 20) from the verse ״וַיָּקוּמוּ לְצַחֵק — and they rose to make merry״ (Exodus 32:6). And perhaps it is for this reason that it says ״וַיִּטְחַן — and he ground,״ to test the sin of grinding, that is, forbidden sexual relations, as explained above; and the calf in and of itself was a bitter matter, and defilement cleaved to it — the impurity of idolatry and the impurity of forbidden sexual relations. Therefore it harmed specifically everyone who had served it, for like found like; but to one who had not served it, it did no harm, for nothing of the ban cleaved to him, and the ground gold was to him like any other ordinary gold. And according to our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sotah 20a), a bitter substance was placed in the waters of the sotah, because it is written (Proverbs 5:3–4): ״כִּי נֹפֶת תִּטֹּפְנָה שִׂפְתֵי זָרָה — for the lips of a strange woman drip honey,״ etc., ״וְאַחֲרִיתָהּ מָרָה כַלַּעֲנָה — and her end is bitter as wormwood.״ And the placing of the water in an earthen vessel — this is because every earthen vessel never escapes its designation; so too one who lies with a forbidden woman and begets from her a mamzer is called ״מְעֻוָּת לֹא יוּכַל לִתְקוֹן — warped, it cannot be made straight״ (Hagigah 9a)." "And what is written, <b>״וְהֶעֱמִיד הַכֹּהֵן אֶת הָאִשָּׁה לִפְנֵי ה׳ — and the priest shall stand the woman before YHWH,״</b> even though the standing before YHWH has already been stated — the commentators said regarding this that the first standing refers to the meal-offering. And this is correct. And that Scripture sometimes puts the belly before the thigh and sometimes puts the thigh first — this is because there was something like a miracle within a miracle: for the bitter waters diminish the flesh, and when her belly swells, it appears as though the flesh increased. Therefore in the two verses where he used the language of bitter waters, he juxtaposed the swelling of the belly; but in the first verse, where he did not mention bitterness, he put the thigh first, for that is where he began with the transgression."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:37.759360080Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}